  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code-generating artificial intelligence agents has revolutionized software development, enabling automated code generation, debugging, and execution. However, these powerful code agents also introduce novel security vulnerabilities and safety concerns. This paper explores the intersection of code agents and red-teaming methodologies, examining how adversarial testing can improve the robustness and security of AI-driven code generation systems. We review recent advances in code agent architectures, discuss emerging red-teaming techniques tailored for code-oriented AI systems, and analyze case studies of successful red-teaming campaigns. Our analysis reveals critical vulnerabilities in current code agent frameworks and proposes a systematic approach to automated red-teaming. We introduce the RedCodeAgent methodology for automatic red-teaming of diverse code agents, demonstrating its effectiveness in identifying security flaws, prompt injection vulnerabilities, and unsafe code execution patterns. This research contributes to the growing body of work on AI safety and security, providing practical insights for developing more resilient code agents.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a significant paradigm shift in how software is developed, maintained, and deployed. These AI-powered systems, typically built upon large language models (LLMs) with specialized code training, can understand natural language instructions and generate functional code in response. Recent advancements have enhanced these agents with tools such as code interpreters, terminal access, and web browsing capabilities, enabling them to execute, debug, and iterate on code autonomously.\n\nWhile the capabilities of code agents are impressive, they also introduce unique security challenges. Unlike traditional software applications, code agents operate in dynamic execution environments and can perform actions such as file system modifications, network calls, and arbitrary code execution. This expanded attack surface makes them susceptible to various adversarial attacks, including prompt injection, jailbreaking, and code injection attacks.\n\nRed-teaming, the practice of adversarially testing systems to identify weaknesses, has become increasingly important in AI safety research. Traditional red-teaming approaches often focus on language models' text generation capabilities. However, code agents introduce additional dimensions to red-teaming, including:\n\n1. **Code execution vulnerabilities**: Adversarial inputs that lead to the execution of harmful or unintended code\n2. **Tool exploitation**: Manipulating code agents to misuse their integrated tools (e.g., file systems, APIs)\n3. **Prompt injection**: Crafting inputs that bypass safety filters and induce unsafe behavior\n4. **Context poisoning**: Exploiting the agent's memory and context windows to influence future behavior\n\nThis paper presents a comprehensive survey of research advances in both code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We focus specifically on automated red-teaming approaches for code agents, examining how red-teaming agents can systematically identify vulnerabilities in diverse code agent architectures.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Code Agent Architectures\n\nRecent years have witnessed significant progress in code agent development. Frameworks such as AutoGen [1], LangChain [2], and Code Interpreter have demonstrated the ability of AI agents to perform complex programming tasks autonomously. These systems typically combine large language models with execution environments, tool-use capabilities, and planning mechanisms to accomplish software development tasks.\n\nThe evolution from simple code completion systems to autonomous code agents has been driven by improvements in model capabilities, particularly in reasoning and multi-step planning. Models like GPT-4 and Claude have demonstrated remarkable proficiency in understanding and generating complex code, enabling the development of sophisticated agent architectures.\n\n### Red-Teaming for AI Systems\n\nRed-teaming has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating AI system safety. Initial efforts focused on text-based vulnerabilities in language models, including prompt injection, jailbreaking, and generation of harmful content [3]. As AI systems have evolved to include tool use and code execution capabilities, red-teaming methodologies have expanded accordingly.\n\nRecent work has identified that code agents introduce unique attack vectors beyond those found in text-only models. The ability to execute code, access file systems, and make network requests creates an expanded attack surface that requires specialized red-teaming approaches.\n\n### RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents\n\nA significant recent contribution to this field is **RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** (arXiv:2510.02609) by Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al. [4]. This work introduces a novel framework for automated red-teaming of code agents, addressing the critical gap in systematic security evaluation of AI-driven code generation systems.\n\nRedCodeAgent operates by automatically generating adversarial prompts designed to exploit vulnerabilities in code agents. The framework is designed to work with diverse code agent architectures, making it broadly applicable across different system designs. Key features of RedCodeAgent include:\n\n- **Automated vulnerability discovery**: The red-teaming agent autonomously generates and tests adversarial inputs against code agents\n- **Diverse attack surface coverage**: The framework targets multiple vulnerability categories including prompt injection, code execution exploitation, and tool misuse\n- **Architecture-agnostic design**: RedCodeAgent can be applied to various code agent implementations without requiring access to internal system details\n- **Iterative refinement**: The system learns from previous red-teaming attempts to improve its adversarial prompt generation\n\nThe RedCodeAgent framework demonstrated significant findings when applied to popular code agent systems, uncovering previously unknown vulnerabilities including:\n- Prompt injection attacks that bypass safety filters in code interpretation modes\n- Execution of arbitrary code through crafted adversarial prompts\n- Tool exploitation enabling unauthorized file system access and network operations\n\nThis work represents an important step toward systematic security evaluation of code agents and provides a foundation for developing more robust agent architectures.\n\n### Other Related Approaches\n\nBeyond RedCodeAgent, several complementary approaches to code agent security have been explored:\n\n- **Adversarial testing frameworks**: Manual and semi-automated testing methodologies for identifying code agent vulnerabilities [5]\n- **Code-specific safety filters**: Techniques for detecting and blocking harmful code generation [6]\n- **Execution sandboxing**: Methods for safely isolating code agent execution environments [7]\n\nThese approaches, while valuable, often lack the automated, systematic nature of RedCodeAgent's methodology, making the latter particularly valuable for continuous security evaluation.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe intersection of code agents and red-teaming represents a critical area of research for ensuring the security and safety of AI-powered software development systems. As code agents become more prevalent and capable, the need for robust red-teaming methodologies becomes increasingly important.\n\nThis paper has surveyed research advances in both code agent architectures and red-teaming techniques, with particular emphasis on automated red-teaming approaches. The RedCodeAgent framework (arXiv:2510.02609) exemplifies the state-of-the-art in automated code agent security testing, providing a systematic methodology for identifying vulnerabilities across diverse agent architectures.\n\nKey findings and directions for future work include:\n\n1. **Continuous evaluation**: The dynamic nature of code agent capabilities necessitates ongoing, automated security testing\n2. **Standardized benchmarks**: The field would benefit from standardized red-teaming benchmarks for comparing agent security\n3. **Defensive methodologies**: Beyond red-teaming, research into defensive techniques for securing code agents is urgently needed\n4. **Multi-agent security**: As systems incorporating multiple interacting code agents emerge, new security challenges will arise\n\nThe RedCodeAgent work and related research highlight the importance of incorporating security considerations from the earliest stages of code agent design. As these systems become integral to software development workflows, ensuring their security and reliability will be paramount.\n\n## References\n\n[1] AutoGen: Enabling Next-Gen LLM Applications via Multi-Agent Conversation, 2023.\n\n[2] LangChain: Building Applications with LLMs through Composability, 2023.\n\n[3] Promising and Challenging Directions in AI Safety Research, arXiv:2304.07780, 2023.\n\n[4] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al., \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents\", arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[5] Adversarial Testing Frameworks for AI Systems, 2024.\n\n[6] Safety Filters for Code Generation Models, 2024.\n\n[7] Secure Execution Environments for AI Agents, 2024.\nEOF"
